WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.836
Two query plans can return the exact same answer and cost wildly different amounts of IO.

00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:09.672
The optimizer's job is to find the cheap one.

00:00:09.672 --> 00:00:13.508
So let us walk through two plans for one query, goal by goal, and watch where the cost comes from.

00:00:13.508 --> 00:00:17.344
The method never changes.

00:00:17.344 --> 00:00:21.180
Add up the cost of every step.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.751
The query finds highly recommended songs.

00:00:24.751 --> 00:00:28.321
Join the Recommendations table to Listens on the pair user i d and song i d, so a match means the user actually listened to a song that was recommended to them.

00:00:28.321 --> 00:00:31.892
Then group by song i d to count the recommendations, and order by that count, highest first.

00:00:31.892 --> 00:00:35.463
So three goals, in any plan.

00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:39.033
Join the two tables.

00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:42.604
Group by song i d.

00:00:42.604 --> 00:00:46.175
Sort by the count.

00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:49.745
Every plan has to hit all three.

00:00:49.745 --> 00:00:53.316
What changes is the algorithm at each step.

00:00:53.316 --> 00:00:56.467
Plan one.

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.619
Goal one, the join.

00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:02.770
A sort merge join over Recommendations and Listens.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.921
To merge, it sorts both tables on the join keys, song i d and user i d, so the joined rows come out already sorted on both.

00:01:05.921 --> 00:01:09.072
That sorted result is table three.

00:01:09.072 --> 00:01:12.224
Hold on to that order.

00:01:12.224 --> 00:01:15.375
It is about to pay off.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.128
Goal two.

00:01:18.128 --> 00:01:20.882
Group by song i d to count the recommendations.

00:01:20.882 --> 00:01:23.635
And here is the payoff.

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:26.388
Song i d is the first key in that sort order, so every song's rows already sit together.

00:01:26.388 --> 00:01:29.141
Scan once and count.

00:01:29.141 --> 00:01:31.895
No extra sort.

00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:34.648
That gives table four, each song i d with its count.

00:01:34.648 --> 00:01:37.519
Goal three.

00:01:37.519 --> 00:01:40.391
Order by that count.

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:43.262
One final Big Sort on the count column, and plan one is done.

00:01:43.262 --> 00:01:46.134
The sorted join back at goal one is what made the grouping free.

00:01:46.134 --> 00:01:49.005
So plan one only ever sorts twice.

00:01:49.005 --> 00:01:51.877
Once inside the join, once here at the end.

00:01:51.877 --> 00:01:55.002
Plan two.

00:01:55.002 --> 00:01:58.126
Same query, different join.

00:01:58.126 --> 00:02:01.251
Goal one is a hash partition join.

00:02:01.251 --> 00:02:04.376
Hash both tables on song i d and user i d into partitions, four of them here, then join the matching ones.

00:02:04.376 --> 00:02:07.501
The join itself is often cheaper than sorting.

00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:10.625
But look at table three.

00:02:10.625 --> 00:02:13.750
It is split on both keys together.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.380
Goal two is where plan two pays.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.010
Group by needs song i d alone, but table three is partitioned on the pair.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.640
So before grouping, it has to re-partition table three on song i d by itself.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.270
Call that table three b.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.900
That extra hash partition is a step plan one never ran.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.530
Then scan and count into table four.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.466
Goal three is identical to plan one.

00:02:39.466 --> 00:02:43.401
One final Big Sort on the count.

00:02:43.401 --> 00:02:47.337
So plan two tied plan one at the join and at the sort, but paid one extra re-partition in the middle.

00:02:47.337 --> 00:02:51.273
That single step is the whole difference between them.

00:02:51.273 --> 00:02:54.784
So which plan wins?

00:02:54.784 --> 00:02:58.295
You do not argue about it, you add it up.

00:02:58.295 --> 00:03:01.806
Both totals are built from the same pieces.

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:05.317
They share the final Big Sort and the write of table four.

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:08.827
The only difference is the join, plus that one extra re-partition plan two pays.

00:03:08.827 --> 00:03:12.338
So plug in the real table sizes, total both, and take the smaller number.

00:03:12.338 --> 00:03:15.849
That is all a cost based optimizer does.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:19.360
It estimates the IO of every plan from formulas like these, and keeps the cheapest.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.871
The trade off is just intuition for which term wins.

00:03:22.871 --> 00:03:26.382
The math is what settles it.

00:03:26.382 --> 00:03:30.654
The cost of a plan is the sum of its steps, nothing more.

00:03:30.654 --> 00:03:34.927
Same answer, different total, and the whole difference here is one re-partition.

00:03:34.927 --> 00:03:39.199
The deciding question is whether the join leaves its output in an order the next step can reuse.

00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:43.472
When it does, the group by is free.

00:03:43.472 --> 00:03:47.744
When it does not, you pay to reshape it.

00:03:47.744 --> 00:03:52.017
So far we costed these plans ourselves.

00:03:52.017 --> 00:03:56.289
Next, we read what a real optimizer chose, with the Postgres explain command.
